    <DataNode elementId="STEAROYL-COA" textLabel="stearoyl-CoA" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="SIARJEKBADXQJG-LFZQUHGESA-J" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="721.5162353515625" centerY="490.130615234375" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">Stearate is a saturated fatty acid that occurs in many animal and vegetable fats and oils. It is a large constituent in milk fats, lard, and cocoa and shea butters. The name comes from Greek for &quot;hard fat&quot;, reflecting the fact that stearate forms a waxy solid |CITS: [16477801]|. Stearate was first described by Michel Eugene Chevreul in 1823. In cells |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| is usually found in the form of |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs stearoyl-[acp]|, |FRAME: STEAROYL-COA| or |FRAME: Stearoyl-lipid &quot;incorporated into lipids&quot;|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="STEAROYL-COA" />
